---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { downloads, site } from '../config';

const anyStore = downloads.play.available || downloads.appStore.available;
---

<Page title="Get the app" description={`Download ${site.name} for Android and iPhone.`}>
  <article class="wrap narrow prose">
    <h1>Get the app</h1>
    <p class="updated">Android and iPhone. Free, and no phone number needed.</p>

    {
      !anyStore && (
        <p class="notice">
          Cuckoo is not in the app stores yet. Until it is, these are the ways in.
          Both are the real app, and both update the same way once the store
          listings go live.
        </p>
      )
    }

    <div class="platforms">
      <div class="platform">
        <h2>Android</h2>
        {
          downloads.play.available ? (
            <a class="button primary" href={downloads.play.url} rel="noopener">
              Get it on Google Play
            </a>
          ) : downloads.android.available ? (
            <a class="button primary" href={downloads.android.url} rel="noopener">
              {downloads.android.label}
            </a>
          ) : (
            <p class="pending">
              A downloadable build is coming. Watch{' '}
              <a href={`${site.repo}/releases`} rel="noopener">the releases page</a>.
            </p>
          )
        }
        <p class="fineprint">Android 8 and later.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="platform">
        <h2>iPhone</h2>
        {
          downloads.appStore.available ? (
            <a class="button primary" href={downloads.appStore.url} rel="noopener">
              Download on the App Store
            </a>
          ) : downloads.ios.available ? (
            <a class="button primary" href={downloads.ios.url} rel="noopener">
              Join the TestFlight
            </a>
          ) : (
            <p class="pending">A TestFlight build is coming.</p>
          )
        }
        <p class="fineprint">iOS 16 and later.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Signing in</h2>
    <p>
      An email address makes an account. The app sends a six-digit code and that
      is the whole of it. There is no password to forget and no phone number to
      give. One device at a time for now, so signing in somewhere new signs the
      old one out.
    </p>

    <h2>Scanned a code and landed here?</h2>
    <p>
      Somebody gave you a link to an agent. Install the app, sign in, then open
      that link again and it will offer to add the agent to your chat list. You
      can block or remove it at any time afterwards.
    </p>

    <h2>Building an agent instead?</h2>
    <p>
      You do not need the app to write one, though it is the easiest way to get
      your first key. Start with the <a href="/docs/quickstart/">quickstart</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Page>

<style>
  .notice {
    border: 1px solid var(--ck-hairline);
    border-radius: 14px;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    color: var(--ck-text-secondary);
  }

  .platforms {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
    margin: 2rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .platform {
    border: 1px solid var(--ck-hairline);
    border-radius: 18px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }

  .platform h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
    border: 0;
    padding: 0;
    font-size: 1.125rem;
  }

  .pending,
  .fineprint {
    color: var(--ck-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .fineprint {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
